# Python cache and bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# SQLite database file, and the sidecars SQLite keeps beside it.
#
# The -shm/-wal rules are not tidiness: a WAL file holds transactions not yet
# folded into the .db, so it carries the same real lead PII the database does.
# `*.db` does not match `foo.db-wal`, so every backup taken by the migration
# scripts left two unignored files sitting in the repo root, ready for the
# first `git add -A` to publish.
pipeline.db
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Environment files
.env
.env.*

# Local auth/session state
auth/

# Cached emails and logs. email_attachments/ holds real customer spreadsheets
# and email_messages/ holds whole exported emails -- same third-party PII the
# input/ rule below exists for.
email_cache/
email_attachments/
email_messages/
logs/

# Real lead data (contains third-party PII) -- never commit actual lead
# spreadsheets, only code that processes them
input/

# Reference documents shown on the dashboard's Reference tab: internal sales
# material (territory maps, price lists) that does not belong in the repo.
# The folder and its README stay tracked so the drop-folder still exists on a
# fresh clone.
reference/*
!reference/README.md

dashboard.log

# Editor files
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Ad-hoc Oracle inventory / report dumps written by the scratch_* scripts.
# Same third-party PII as input/ -- real customer names, companies and Oracle
# lead numbers -- and `*.json` / `*.xlsx` are otherwise tracked file types, so
# these need naming explicitly or a `git add -A` publishes them.
scratch_*.json
scratch_*.xlsx

# Data exports from transfer_data.py: the whole customer dataset in one file.
# export refuses to write inside the repo, but the zip is often copied here to
# be imported.
*.zip
*.zip.part
